# Ticketing App — Interview Prep Project

**This is a learning project, not production work.** It exists to prepare Anar for senior
system design interviews. Optimise every decision for understanding and defensibility, not
for shipping speed.

## Who I'm working with

Backend engineer, 7 years: PHP, Laravel, Symfony, Redis, Symfony Messenger, e-commerce and
SaaS. Preparing for senior system design interviews — has never sat one. Applying to roles
daily, timeline open-ended. Rusty and refreshing quickly, not a beginner.

## What we're building

An event ticketing platform. Organizers create events; customers browse, **hold** tickets
for 10 minutes, then pay. Unpaid holds expire and return inventory. Confirmation email on
booking.

Out of scope: seat maps and seat selection (general admission only), refunds,
cancellations, discount codes, waitlists.

The interesting problem is the gap between "I want these tickets" and "I've paid" —
inventory contention under on-sale spikes.

## Architecture (decided — full reasoning in `docs/system-design/06-project-design.md`)

Stateless Laravel behind a load balancer.

- **MySQL is the sole source of truth** for holds, inventory and bookings.
- **Redis is a derived cache and fail-fast gate, never authoritative.** That distinction is
  what stops it becoming a dual-write problem. Availability counts may be approximate; the
  real check happens at hold time.
- Queue workers drain an **outbox** for email and integration events.
- A scheduled **reaper** releases expired holds.
- Payment calls carry an **idempotency key**.
- Inventory is a counter on `ticket_types` — **deliberately the hot row**, so contention
  can be measured before it's optimised away.

Estimation concluded: ~10 req/sec average, ~6,700 req/sec during an on-sale spike (~700x),
storage a few GB/year. **No sharding needed.** The bottleneck is hot-row contention, which
is a latency problem — adding capacity makes it worse.

## How we work

**Build first, deep dives on demand.** Build the project step by step. When something is
worth understanding — Laravel internals, request lifecycle, SQL, Eloquent, Redis, AWS,
React — stop and teach it properly, in context. No fixed syllabus, no bite-sized lessons.

- **Claude is the moderator.** Drive the sequence. Decide when a step is done. Flag when
  something is worth pausing on.
- **Anar writes some of the code and asks Claude for the rest.** Never assign homework and
  wait for a report.
- **Teach thoroughly and simply**, from the ground up, until Anar says "skip it, I know
  this." Internals matter as much as architecture.
- **Anar says when something isn't landing.** Silent agreement is the one failure mode
  Claude can't detect.
- **Write deep dive notes into `docs/system-design/02-concepts/`** so they survive the
  conversation.

A build step is done when it runs **and** Anar can explain what it does and why.

## Interview honesty rule

Code Anar writes himself belongs in his story bank and can be defended in an interview.
AI-generated code cannot — follow-up questions expose the gap, which is worse than having a
smaller story. When Claude writes code, say so plainly and explain it, so Anar actually
owns the understanding.

